      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-21T15:28:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This logic was meant to preserve the original behavior where the copies would never go outside src and dst.\n\nI am wondering if we should update it to just make sure we never go outside dst? glBlitFramebuffer and the vulkan equivalent are defined to use kClamp if the src coordinates go beyond the src content, and draw fallbacks can be used when those aren\u0027t available.\n\nThe main benefit of this is that we can assume that the full dstRect content is filled in by the copy. Even before, if srcRect is clipped, the dstRect shrinks to leave some pixels unfilled by the copy. Since this happens inside the copy task, and doesn\u0027t fail the task, nothing gets bubbled up to the caller to indicate that some extra region of dst needs to be cleared/initialized.\n\nWhat do others think?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 47,
        "endChar": 63
      },
      "revId": "46df8e5a499908f8860c085455e5bab21fbe4d9f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b1f76cd7_d6c35afd",
        "filename": "src/gpu/ganesh/geometry/GrRect.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 47,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-21T16:07:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m wondering if we just should have a new function that operates on float rects. This seems a little less well defined. We\u0027re assuming we should round out both rects which could change the scale factors. If the in/out rects were float then the caller could decide whether and how to integralize.",
      "parentUuid": "e4a608a2_561886e4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 47,
        "endChar": 63
      },
      "revId": "46df8e5a499908f8860c085455e5bab21fbe4d9f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ac98fd1d_06a9331b",
        "filename": "src/gpu/ganesh/geometry/GrRect.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 47,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-22T14:31:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "After thinking through Brian\u0027s comment and my first comment, I think we should:\n1. Keep GrClipSrcRectAndDstPoint as-is\n2. Add a GrClipSrcRectAndDstRect that works on floating point\n3. Update the copy() APIs and GrCopyTask to require that srcRect and dstRect are already contained in the surfaces.\n4. Callers will use the appropriate function from 1 or 2 to meet 3\u0027s requirements and they can handle rounding, or what to do if dstRect would shrink and they needed to fill.\n\nWhile there\u0027s some benefit to using copies to clamp the srcRect sampling and fill out dstRect, it\u0027s only available on some platforms, and gut-feeling says it\u0027s where driver bugs will lie. And the scaling use case that this will be used for in backdrop filters and rescaleInto() construct srcRect and dstRect such that they shouldn\u0027t be getting clipped anyways.\n\n -\u003e So that could also mean we don\u0027t even bother adding GrClipSrcRectAndDstRect at this time, and just change where GrCliPSrcRectAndDstPoint is called so that rescaleInto() can just provide it\u0027s valid rects directly?",
      "parentUuid": "b1f76cd7_d6c35afd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 43,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 47,
        "endChar": 63
      },
      "revId": "46df8e5a499908f8860c085455e5bab21fbe4d9f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
